---
export const prerender = true

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import HeadingWithBackground from '../components/HeadingWithBackground.astro'
import EmailLink from '../components/EmailLink.astro'
import { roles } from '../utility/constants'

const { president, giftShop } = roles;
---

<Layout subtitle="Store">
	<HeadingWithBackground
		background="noseHill2"
	>
		<h1>Store</h1>
	</HeadingWithBackground>

	<section>
		<h2>T-Shirts</h2>
		<section class="sale-item" id="tshirt-2026">
			<h3>2026 Alberta Palaeontological Society T-Shirt</h3>
			<div class="item-content">
				<div class="item-picture">
					<img
						src="/store/2026 T shirt image close up on medium.jpg"
						alt="White t-shirt printed with the 2026 Alberta Palaeontological Society design"
						style="width: 80%;"
					/>
				</div>

				<p>
					Our newest t-shirt is a colour design by Maxwell Bley, printed
					on white fabric.
				</p>
				<p>
					The circular emblem is lettered “Alberta Palaeontological
					Society” and “Since 1986.” A green map of Alberta, marked
					with a fossil bone, sits among mountains and pines, with a
					small mammal and a bird above a shark, fish, an ammonite,
					and a crinoid.
				</p>
				<p>
					Shirts are available in adult S, M, L, XL, and XXL, and in
					youth S and L. They fit slightly large, and some sizes may
					have limited stock. APS members: $15. Non-members: $20.
				</p>
				<p>
					To order, fill out the <a
						href="/store/T-shirt 2026 design order form PDF Fillable20261004.pdf"
						>2026 t-shirt order form</a
					> and email it to {giftShop.name} at <EmailLink
						email="giftshop@albertapaleo.org"
					/>. These can also be purchased at our <a
						href="/events/monthlymeetings">meetings</a
					>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<br />

		<section class="sale-item" id="tshirt">
			<h3>2023 Alberta Palaeontological Society T-Shirt</h3>
			<div class="item-content">
				<div class="item-picture">
					<img
						src="/announcement-media/APSShirt2-shirt-cropped.jpg"
						alt="Forest green t-shirt printed with the 2023 Alberta Palaeontological Society design"
						style="width: 80%;"
					/>
				</div>

				<p>
					This design by our President, {president.name}, is printed in
					white on forest green fabric. It is available in adult S, M,
					L, XL, and XXL. APS members: $15. Non-members: $20.
				</p>
				<p>In his own words,</p>

				<blockquote>
					"The new t-shirt design is meant to celebrate the diversity
					of Alberta’s prehistory and connections with our
					neighbouring regions. Flanking the APS logo to the left and
					right are a <em>Pachyrhinosaurus lakustai</em> from Pipestone
					Creek and a <em>Bison latifrons</em>, the ice-age longhorn
					buffalo that ranged across North America. Beneath the <em
						>Pachyrhinosaurus</em
					>
					is <em>Albertonectes vanderveldei</em> from the Bearpaw Formation,
					which is the world’s longest known elasmosaur. Beneath the
					<em>Bison</em> are a representative rugose coral, brachiopod,
					and scaphite ammonite. Directly beneath the APS logo are stromatolites,
					referencing the 1.1-billion-year-old Siyeh Formation fossils
					found in the Waterton-Glacier International Peace Park and Castle
					Wildland Provincial Park area. Above the <em
						>Pachyrhinosaurus</em
					> is a giant lycopod tree like <em>Lepidodendron</em> or <em
						>Sigillaria</em
					>, representing the
					<em>Stigmaria</em> site that used to exist in Canyon Creek. <em
						>Stigmaria</em
					>
					is the name assigned to the root fossils of these ancient lycopods.
					One of less than a handful of <em>Stigmaria</em> sites in Canada,
					the site in Canyon Creek was apparently wiped out by the 2013
					floods. Above the <em>Bison</em> is a <em>Metasequoia</em> tree,
					the leaves, twigs, and cones of which are common Late Cretaceous
					and Early Cenozoic fossils in Alberta. Between them is a volcano
					representing the 110-million-year-old eruption in the Cranbrook,
					B.C., area that deposited the Crowsnest Volcanics here in Alberta.
					Blairemorite is a rare volcanic rock known only from the Crowsnest
					Volcanics site in the Crowsnest Pass and the Lupata Gorge in
					Mozambique. The volcano is also a tribute to late APS member
					Phil Benham, who had a passion for climbing volcanoes around
					the world and passed away earlier this year."
				</blockquote>

				<p>
					To order, fill out the <a
						href="/store/T-shirt 2023 design order form 20260525.pdf"
						>2023 t-shirt order form</a
					> and email it to {giftShop.name} at <EmailLink
						email="giftshop@albertapaleo.org"
					/>. These can also be purchased at our <a
						href="/events/monthlymeetings">meetings</a
					>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Books</h2>
		<section class="sale-item" id="commonvertebratefossils">
			<h3>
				Book 1 - Guide to Common Vertebrate Fossils from the Cretaceous
				of Alberta
			</h3>
			<div class="item-content">
				<div class="item-picture">
					<img src="guidetofossilssmall.jpg" />
				</div>

				<p>
					Use this book to identify your fossils using Hope Johnson's
					and other illustrators' detailed and labelled technical
					renderings of the dinosaurs, fish, lizards, amphibians,
					marine reptiles and turtles from the Alberta Cretaceous.
					This is a valuable guide for amateur fossil enthusiasts
					which includes 234 pages and illustrated with 144 drawings
					in a coil binding for ease of use.
				</p>

				<p>
					Included here is a link to several <a
						href="/store/booksamplepages.pdf">sample pages</a
					> that are a good representation of the content.
				</p>

				<p>
					To order a copy, fill out the <a href="/store/book1.pdf"
						>book 1 order form</a
					> and email it to {giftShop.name} at <EmailLink
						email="giftshop@albertapaleo.org"
					/>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<br />

		<section class="item-sale-item" id="hopejohnson">
			<h3>
				Book 2 - Now There Was a Lady!, Hope Johnson, LL.D., 1916 - 2010
				<span class="out-of-print-span">
					<em>Out of Print</em>
				</span>
			</h3>
			<div class="item-content">
				<div class="item-picture">
					<img src="ladycoversmall.jpg" />
				</div>
				<p>
					<b
						>By Darren Tanke, edited and published by the Alberta
						Palaeontological Society,</b
					>
				</p>
				<p>
					with forewords by palaeontologist Dr. Philip J. Currie,
					artist Allan Jensen and geologist, museologist, naturalist
					and writer, David A.E. Spalding.
				</p>
				<p>
					The 2010 passing of Hope Johnson marked the end of an era
					for Alberta’s verte­brate palaeontology communities. Her
					death affected other disciplines, too, as she travelled in
					many circles within the province for 65 years. How many
					among us can truly say they never knew her personally, saw
					her art work, or learned to identify Alberta prairie plants,
					or Late Cretaceous bones and teeth through her fossil
					identification books? During much of her middle and later
					life, and especially during the late 1950s to 1980s, Hope
					was a well-known and respected powerhouse in the Albertan
					amateur and professional vertebrate palae­ontological
					communities. She was also heavily involved in the naturalist
					and visual arts communities as well as charitable
					organizations. This book focuses on her extensive activities
					in Alberta vertebrate palaeontology and provides examples of
					some of her fossil and botanical drawings and paintings.
					Coil bound, 283 pages; extensively illustrated in black and
					white, with 30-page colour section showcasing Hope Johnson’s
					art. Includes index.
				</p>
				<p class="out-of-print-p">
					This book is currently out of print, but individual copies can be printed on demand. Pricing will reflect the cost of printing single copies. Contact <a href="mailto:giftshop@albertapaleo.org?subject=Ordering Howard Johnson Book">
						{giftShop.name}
					</a> if you are interested.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
	</section>

	<h2>To Order</h2>
	<p>
		Fill out the appropriate form and email it to {giftShop.name} at <EmailLink
			email="giftshop@albertapaleo.org"
		/>. The 2026 t-shirt form can be completed on a computer and emailed as
		a PDF. For the other forms, scan or photograph the completed form and
		attach it to your email. Pay by Interac e-Transfer to the same address,
		and name the items you are ordering in the message field.
	</p>

	<p>
		You can also print the form and mail it with a cheque or money order to
		the address on the form.
	</p>

	<p>
		To obtain cost of shipping, domestic or international, please contact
		{giftShop.name} at <EmailLink email="giftshop@albertapaleo.org" />.
	</p>

	<p>
		To save the cost of shipping, you may also purchase a copy directly at
		any <a href="/events/"> APS event</a>, including at our <a
			href="/events/monthlymeetings/"
		>
			monthly meetings.
		</a> We will have copies on hand available for sale.
	</p>

	<style>
		div.item-content {
			display: flex;
			align-items: flex-start;
			flex-direction: column;
		}
		div.item-picture {
			align-self: center;
			/* float: left; */
		}
		img {
			margin: calc(4 * var(--theme-spacing-base));
		}
		section.sale-item {
			display: inline-block;
		}
		.out-of-print-span {
			color: var(--error-colour-medium);
			padding-left: calc(2 * var(--theme-spacing-base));
		}
		.out-of-print-p {
		color: var(--error-colour-medium);
		}

		/* desktop view */
		@media screen and (min-width: 768px) {
			div.item-content {
				display: block;
			}
			div.item-picture {
				align-self: revert;
				float: left;
			}
			img {
				margin: 0 calc(4 * var(--theme-spacing-base))
					calc(4 * var(--theme-spacing-base))
					calc(4 * var(--theme-spacing-base));
			}
		}
	</style>
</Layout>
